            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "which we now call π (we say ‘pi’ as in ‘pie’ and not as in ‘pizza’), represents a bridge between many different areas of mathematics, and connects the straight-edged world of polygons with the infinite curves of nature. While early civilisations relied on practical, ʻgood-enoughʼ values for construction and trade, the pursuit of this ratio eventually became an important pastime of mathematicians that helped to benchmark the sophistication of mathematical theories."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.243,
            0.886,
            0.354
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Mesopotamia (c. 1900 BCE), mathematicians moved beyond the crude integer value of 3. They realised that a circle had a perimeter that was slightly larger than the hexagon inscribed within it. They concluded that π should therefore be larger than 3, and they set the value of π to be "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "3 + { \\frac { 1 } { 8 } } = 3 . 1 2 5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.392,
            0.346,
            0.607,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.392,
                0.346,
                0.607,
                0.499
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_3_image_4.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.242,
                0.504,
                0.753,
                0.542
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 6.6 The Mesopotamian Hexagon-to-Circle comparison. Can you see why this shows that "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\pi \\geq 3 ?"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.554,
            0.886,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "By "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "250 BCE, Archimedes of Syracuse",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " brought a new level of rigor to the problem. He ‘trapped’ the value of π between the perimeters of inscribed and circumscribed polygons. By using both an inscribed and circumscribed hexagon, Archimedes showed that π is between 3 and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "2 { \\sqrt { 3 } } \\approx 3 . 4 6"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Working his way up to 96-sided polygons, Archimedes found that 3 "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac{10}{71} < \\pi < 3\\frac{1}{7}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.393,
            0.692,
            0.606,
            0.821
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
